# delegate: let a Dagger coding agent hand a self-contained subtask to a fresh
# sub-agent and get back only the distilled result.
#
# The motivating case (from a real session retro): a noisy, context-heavy loop
# — "build the CLI, boot the engine, run the agent, poke the TUI, report just
# the final screen and whether it panicked" — dumps thousands of lines of build
# and boot output into the parent's context for two useful bits of signal. A
# sub-agent absorbs all that: it runs the loop in ITS OWN conversation (rolled
# up under this tool call in the TUI, see the conversation nesting in
# dagql/dagui/conversation.go) and hands back a single concise report.
#
# Modeled on ./modules/tui-qa (the @agent-tools shape). The sub-agent is a fresh
# root `llm` conversation (Query.llm — inherits the session's default model and
# auth) bound to the same Workspace as the parent (auto-filled; see
# core/llm_object_tools.go), so it has the same tools. It runs to completion via
# `loop` and we surface its `lastReply`.
#
# The sub-agent shares the parent's WORKSPACE, not its session: composing the
# workspace's agents builds fresh module instances, so none of the
# parent's live session state comes along — no running engine-lab engine, no
# live tui-qa TUI session, no bound-object state (e.g. engine = null). Tools
# that hold live state start fresh and would have to rebuild.
#
# Two flavors:
#   - `delegate` reports back in text only: investigative / QA / analysis
#     legwork. The sub-agent's own workspace edits are NOT merged into yours
#     (the tool returns a String, not a Changeset).
#   - `delegateEdits` returns the sub-agent's filesystem changes as a
#     Changeset. Changeset-returning tools are the engine's parallel-writes
#     path: CallBatch runs them concurrently against the same workspace and
#     merges the results with a git octopus merge before applying them (see
#     core/mcp.go CallBatch + core/changeset.go WithChangesets), so several
#     delegated editors can safely work at once.
"""
Offload a self-contained subtask to a fresh sub-agent and get back only its
final report — or, with delegateEdits, its workspace changes.
"""
type Delegate {
  """
  Compose the delegate tool onto a base LLM.
  """
  agent(base: LLM!): LLM! @agent {
    base
      .withTools(currentNode)
      .withSystemPrompt(systemPrompt)
  }

  """
  Delegate a self-contained subtask to a fresh sub-agent and return only its
  final answer.

  The sub-agent starts a brand-new conversation bound to your workspace (so it
  has the same tools you do), works the task autonomously to completion, and
  hands back a single concise result — all of its intermediate tool calls and
  chatter stay in the sub-agent's own context (rolled up under this call)
  instead of flooding yours. Reach for it to offload noisy, context-heavy, or
  well-scoped legwork — e.g. "build and run the agent, drive the TUI, and report
  just the final screen and whether it panicked" — and keep your own context
  focused. The sub-agent shares your WORKSPACE, not your session: tools that
  hold live state (engine-lab's engine, tui-qa's TUI session) start fresh and
  would have to rebuild — so either delegate tasks that are cheap to set up,
  or pass any needed endpoints/details explicitly in the task text.
  Note: this tool does NOT merge the sub-agent's workspace edits back,
  so use it for investigation/QA/analysis; use `delegateEdits` to farm out
  editing work.
  """
  delegate(
    """
    The subtask for the sub-agent. Be specific about what to do AND exactly what
    to report back, since only its final reply returns to you.
    """
    task: String!,
    source: Workspace!,
  ): String! {
    source
      .agents(exclude: ["delegate"])
      .compose
      .withSystemPrompt(subAgentPrompt)
      .withPrompt(task)
      .loop
      .lastReply
  }

  """
  Delegate a self-contained EDITING subtask to a fresh sub-agent working in a
  nested sandbox, and merge the changes it makes back into your filesystem.

  The sub-agent runs in its own sandbox seeded with a copy of your workspace
  (and the same tools you have), works the task autonomously to completion,
  and this tool returns the changes it made to its sandbox's filesystem as a
  changeset. That changeset is then applied to YOUR filesystem — merged safely
  even when several delegated editors run in parallel, via a git octopus
  merge. This makes it suitable for farming out distinct tasks and work
  streams concurrently, at least to the extent that they don't step on each
  other's toes TOO much: edits to unrelated files always merge cleanly, while
  conflicting edits to the same lines fail the merge. The sub-agent's final
  report is surfaced above the patch summary.

  The sub-agent shares your WORKSPACE, not your session: tools that hold live
  state (engine-lab's engine, tui-qa's TUI session) start fresh in its sandbox
  and would have to rebuild — so keep delegated edits cheap to set up, or pass
  any needed endpoints/details explicitly in the task text.
  """
  delegateEdits(
    """
    The editing subtask for the sub-agent. Be specific about what to change
    and where, and what to report back — the sub-agent starts fresh with none
    of your conversation context.
    """
    task: String!,
    source: Workspace!,
  ): Changeset! {
    let worker = source
      .agents(exclude: ["delegate"])
      .compose
      .withSystemPrompt(editorSubAgentPrompt)
      .withPrompt(task)
      .loop
    # Surface the sub-agent's report alongside the returned changeset (tool
    # routing prepends print output to the patch summary).
    print(worker.lastReply)
    # Only the sub-agent's own delta: its final workspace vs. the snapshot it
    # was handed (which already includes your pending overlay).
    worker.workspace.directory("/").changes(from: source.directory("/"))
  }

  # Instructions for the PARENT agent holding this tool.
  let systemPrompt: String! = """
    You can hand a self-contained subtask to a fresh sub-agent with the
    `delegate` and `delegateEdits` tools. The sub-agent runs in its own
    conversation with the same tools you have, so its intermediate work never
    enters your context.

    - `delegate` returns only the sub-agent's final report; its workspace
      edits are discarded. Use it for investigation, QA, and analysis.
    - `delegateEdits` runs the sub-agent in a nested sandbox and merges the
      filesystem changes it makes back into your workspace (an octopus merge
      keeps parallel editors safe). Use it to farm out distinct editing tasks
      and work streams — several at once is fine, as long as the tasks don't
      step on each other's toes too much.

    The sub-agent shares your WORKSPACE, not your session: tools that hold
    live state (engine-lab's engine, tui-qa's TUI session) start fresh and
    would have to rebuild — so either delegate tasks that are cheap to set
    up, or pass any needed endpoints/details explicitly in the task text.

    Delegate when a subtask is noisy (dumps a lot of output), context-heavy,
    or cleanly scoped — e.g. reproducing a bug end-to-end, exploring an
    unfamiliar subtree, running a build/QA loop, or a well-bounded batch of
    edits — and phrase the task so it says both what to do and exactly what to
    report back. Keep doing the work yourself when it's small or needs your
    accumulated context.
  """

  # Instructions for the SUB-agent spawned by `delegate`.
  let subAgentPrompt: String! = """
    You are a sub-agent handling one self-contained task for another agent.
    Work autonomously to completion using the available tools — do not ask
    follow-up questions. When done, reply with a single concise report covering
    exactly what was asked and nothing more: the outcome, the key evidence
    (relevant output, not raw dumps), and any errors, panics, or surprises.
    Your reply is the ONLY thing that returns to the caller, so make it
    self-contained.
  """

  # Instructions for the SUB-agent spawned by `delegateEdits`.
  let editorSubAgentPrompt: String! = """
    You are a sub-agent handling one self-contained EDITING task for another
    agent. Work autonomously to completion using the available tools — do not
    ask follow-up questions. Make your edits directly in your workspace: when
    you finish, the changes you made to your filesystem are automatically
    returned to the caller and merged into theirs, so do NOT try to export,
    commit, or otherwise deliver them yourself. Other sub-agents may be
    editing sibling parts of the same workspace in parallel, so stay within
    the scope of your task and touch only the files it requires. When done,
    reply with a single concise report: what you changed and why, anything you
    deliberately left alone, and any errors or surprises.
  """
}
